Introduction {#introduction}
Active matter encompasses a wide array of non-equilibrium many-body systems driven by internal energy dissipation, resulting in rich and often counterintuitive collective phenomena. This field is significant both theoretically and experimentally. The broad category of active matter includes various types of active particles, such as self-walking particles , crawling bacteria , swarming birds and fish , and microscopic swimmers like bacteria and pollen grains . Additionally, continuous media known as polar active fluids are defined by a non-vanishing mean polar order parameter in the hydrodynamic limit. These fluids exhibit sustained, disorder-free, coherent motion. A primary area of interest within this domain is the impact of quenched randomness on the dynamics and scaling exponents of the system. This area of study is particularly relevant to the field of biophysics. Previous investigations have examined the influence of noise on one-dimensional ($d=1$) and two-dimensional ($d=2$) polar active fluids, as well as the dynamics of nematic liquid crystals subjected to multiplicative noise. However, analytical approaches to these problems are often hindered by the complexity of the
